.TH IPC 7
.SH NAME
ipc \- messages, ports and replies: how SIEOS's programs talk
.SH DESCRIPTION
Programs talk with
.BR messages .
A server creates a named
.B port
and waits on it.
A client sends a message and sleeps until the reply.
The kernel copies the message directly from one program's memory into the
other's (one copy, no buffering in the kernel) and stamps it with the
sender's identity, which servers can trust.
.SS A message
.nf
typedef struct {
    uint64_t w[4];               /* w[0] = operation, or result */
    const void *sbuf; uint64_t slen;
    void *rbuf;       uint64_t rlen;
    int32_t pid, uid, gid, _pad; /* the sender, filled in by the kernel */
} msg_t;
.fi
Small values travel in w[]; larger data is copied from the sender's sbuf
into the receiver's rbuf (its size comes back in rlen).
.SS The calls
.TP
.BR port_create (2)
a port, named or not; a name is unique while its port lives.
.TP
.BR port_lookup (2)
a port by name; it waits until the port exists
.RB ( port_find :
\-ENOENT at once instead).
.TP
.BR ipc_call (2)
send w[] and sbuf, sleep until the reply, whose w[] and data come back into
the same msg_t.
.TP
.BR ipc_recv (2)
wait for a message on a port: it fills w[], copies the data into rbuf and
returns the sender's
.BR token ,
by which the server answers.
.TP
.BR ipc_reply (2)
answer the message received from a token: w[] and sbuf go back to the
caller.
.TP
.BR ipc_reply_recv (2)
reply, then receive the next message, in one call: the server's fast path.
.PP
A server may keep a token and answer later: a call that must wait (data, a
connection, a key) costs no CPU time meanwhile.
By convention a reply's w[0] is the result, >= 0, or \-errno.
.SS Interrupts and events
A driver binds its device's interrupt to its port
.RB ( irq_bind (2),
.BR irq_msi (2)):
it arrives as a message with token 0, w[0] holding one bit per interrupt
that occurred, so one loop serves hardware and clients.
The driver answers with
.BR irq_ack (2).
The kernel also sends token 0 messages for other events (w[1]):
.B NOTE_CHILD
(a child ended, on the port given to
.BR sys_child_port (2))
and
.B NOTE_WANT
(someone waits for an on-demand server; the supervisor only).
.SS When a server restarts
A server that crashes is started again by
.BR init (8).
A call to it fails with \-EPIPE (it died during the call) or \-ENOENT (the
port number is stale).
The library's
.B call_named
then finds the port again by name (waiting until the new server is up) and
sends the call again: always after \-ENOENT, which never reached the old
server, and after \-EPIPE only for requests safe to do twice (reads, writes
at an offset, console text, most file operations; not a login, nor closing
a file).
Open files are reopened.
.SH EXAMPLES
Printing "hi" from the shell:
.nf
sh:   ipc_call(console, CON_WRITE "hi")  \-> kernel copies "hi" \-> con
con:  ipc_recv returns; prints it; ipc_reply_recv(token, 2) \-> sh goes on
.fi
